---
// How a score is made: the pipeline and its checks, the terms the site uses, the repo's layout
// and the first commands to run.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const repo = "https://github.com/Colombo-HCI-Lab/deeptrace-bench";
const dirs = [
  ["configs", "configs/", "One YAML per model, dataset and evalset; the evaluation rules; weight hashes."],
  ["src/deeptrace_bench", "src/deeptrace_bench/", "The harness: manifests, preprocessing, model adapters, scoring, metrics and the guards."],
  ["scripts", "scripts/", "The commands: setup, score, evaluate, smoke, parity, profile and build_site."],
  ["results", "results/", "Published aggregates, parity summaries and compute profiles. No per-clip data."],
  ["slurm", "slurm/", "The same steps as job templates for the Curnagl cluster."],
  ["patches", "third_party/ · patches/", "Upstream detector code pinned by commit, and the patches it needs."],
  ["docs", "docs/", "This site: the status data and every doc."],
  ["tests", "tests/", "Unit tests on synthetic fixtures only."],
];
---

<Base
  title="How it works · deeptrace-bench"
  description="From a published detector to one trusted number: the pipeline, the checks along it, and the terms this site uses."
  section="method"
>
  <div class="stack">
    <header class="mast">
      <div class="kicker">How it works</div>
      <h1>From a published detector to one trusted number</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every detector and dataset goes through the same five steps under the same rules. Three checks along the way
        decide whether a number counts.
      </p>
    </header>
    <nav class="index" aria-label="On this page">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#pipeline">Pipeline</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms">Key terms</a></li>
        <li><a href="#repo">The repo</a></li>
        <li><a href="#run">Run it yourself</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${base}docs/evaluation/`}>Evaluation in full</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="pipeline" class="block">
      <header>
        <h2>The pipeline</h2>
        <p>Each step is one script. The dashed checks below the steps sit where they act.</p>
      </header>
      <div>
        <div class="flow">
          <div class="stage"><div class="kicker">1 · configs/</div><h3>Configure</h3><p>One file per model (code, weights, what it trained on), per dataset (where to get it, its licence) and per evalset (what gets scored). One shared set of evaluation rules.</p></div>
          <div class="stage"><div class="kicker">2 · setup_*.py</div><h3>Set up</h3><p>Download each model at a fixed version and check its weights. Download each dataset and list every clip with its label, language and generator.</p></div>
          <div class="stage"><div class="kicker">3 · score.py</div><h3>Score</h3><p>Prepare every clip the same way for every model (4&nbsp;s audio windows; detected, cropped faces), run the model, store one fake-score per clip. Resumes after a stop.</p></div>
          <div class="stage"><div class="kicker">4 · evaluate.py</div><h3>Evaluate</h3><p>AUC and EER with 95% intervals, overall and per group (language, generator, gender, race). Failed clips are counted, never dropped.</p></div>
          <div class="stage"><div class="kicker">5 · --publish</div><h3>Publish</h3><p>Only summary tables reach the public repo, each row with its real/fake pairing and the contamination verdict.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="gates">
          <div class="gate g-parity"><strong>Parity check</strong>Our copy of a model must match the authors' code within 0.001 on about 200 clips before its numbers count.</div>
          <div class="gate g-guard"><strong>Contamination guard</strong>A model is never scored on data it trained on. Refused pairs show as ⊘.</div>
          <div class="gate g-pub"><strong>Publish guard</strong>Test runs and anything naming a single clip never reach the public repo.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cols-2" style="margin-top: 8px">
        <div class="where"><strong>In the public repo</strong>Code, settings, documentation, weight fingerprints, summary results.</div>
        <div class="where">
          <strong>In private storage only (DTB_ROOT)</strong>
          <span>Datasets, model weights, clip lists and per-clip scores. Dataset licences don't allow sharing them. <a href={`${base}docs/data_policy/`}>Data policy</a></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="terms" class="block">
      <header>
        <h2>Key terms</h2>
        <p>Every number on this site answers one question: given a clip, can the detector tell whether it is real or fake?</p>
      </header>
      <div class="terms">
        <dl>
          <div>What's tested</div>
          <div><dt>Deepfake</dt><dd>Audio or video made or changed by AI to look or sound like a real person: a cloned voice, a swapped face, speech generated from text.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Detector</dt><dd>A neural network that scores a clip from 0 (real) to 1 (fake). Every detector here comes from a published paper with released weights.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Dataset and evalset</dt><dd>A dataset is a published collection of real and fake clips. An evalset is the exact slice we score: which clips, which languages, which count as real or fake.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Zero-shot</dt><dd>Testing a detector on data unlike its training data, without retraining it. That's the point: how do detectors built elsewhere do on South Asian data?</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <dl>
          <div>How it's scored</div>
          <div>
            <dt>AUC</dt>
            <dd>
              The chance the detector gives a random fake a higher score than a random real clip. Higher is better.
              <div class="scale small" aria-hidden="true">
                <div class="track"></div>
                <div class="ticks"><span>0 backwards</span><span>0.5 coin flip</span><span>1 perfect</span></div>
              </div>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div><dt>EER (equal error rate)</dt><dd>Set the cut-off where the share of real clips called fake equals the share of fakes called real. That shared error rate is the EER. Lower is better; 50% is guessing.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>False-positive rate</dt><dd>The share of real clips wrongly flagged as fake at a fixed cut-off. A detector can rank groups equally well and still raise far more false alarms for one of them.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <dl>
          <div>Why a number counts</div>
          <div><dt>Reproduction</dt><dd>Running a detector on its own paper's dataset to check our numbers match the published ones.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Parity check</dt><dd>Our copy and the authors' original code score the same ~200 clips; the scores must agree within 0.001.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Contamination guard</dt><dd>Compares each detector's training data with every evalset and refuses pairs that overlap (⊘). It compares datasets, not the TTS systems behind them.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Same corpus or cross corpus</dt><dd>Same corpus: real and fake clips come from the same recordings. Cross corpus: from different sources, so a detector could score well by noticing the microphone. Cross-corpus numbers never headline.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="repo" class="block">
      <header>
        <h2>The repo at a glance</h2>
        <p>Where each part of the pipeline lives in <a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colombo-HCI-Lab/deeptrace-bench</a>.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="cards small">
        {
          dirs.map(([path, label, text]) => (
            <a class="card repo" href={`${repo}/tree/main/${path}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              <code>{label}</code>
              <span>{text}</span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="run" class="block">
      <header>
        <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
        <p>
          Needs <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">uv</a>. Linux with CUDA 12 for real
          runs; Apple Silicon is enough for the tests. The smoke test pushes a few clips through every step and writes a
          readable report.
        </p>
      </header>
      <pre class="cmd" tabindex="0"><code>git config core.hooksPath scripts/hooks   <i># refuse commits that contain data</i>
cp .env.example .env                      <i># point DTB_ROOT at the store</i>
uv sync
uv run pytest

uv run scripts/setup_models.py gend --record   <i># GenD and the face detector (~3 GB)</i>
uv run scripts/smoke.py                        <i># the whole pipeline on a few items</i></code></pre>
      <div class="links">
        <a href={`${base}docs/smoke_test/`}>Smoke test →</a>
        <a href={`${base}docs/adding_a_model/`}>Adding a model →</a>
        <a href={`${base}docs/adding_a_dataset/`}>Adding a dataset →</a>
        <a href={`${base}docs/cluster/`}>Running on Curnagl →</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
